NAME
    examine - view and change memory a byte at a time

SYNOPSIS
    examine addr

DESCRIPTION
    Shows the byte at the hexadecimal address addr as

        AAAA: vv

    and waits for a key, read without echo (examine echoes the digits
    it uses):

        Enter            keep the byte, go to the next address
        two hex digits   store that byte, go to the next address
        .  or Ctrl-D     quit to the shell

    Any other key shows the same address again, as does a hex digit
    followed by anything but a second one. "examine: bad address" when
    addr is not hex. The shell's counterpart of the monitor's E
    command. examine is /BIN/EXAMINE.

    There are no guard rails: it writes over the OS ($1000..$4FFF) or
    over itself ($5000 up) as readily as anywhere else. dump views a
    block without the risk of a stray key; dep writes a run of bytes.

EXAMPLES
    examine 6000      Enter steps through memory, . quits
    examine 6000      then 4A stores $4A at $6000

NOTES
    On the instruction-level emulator a lower-case q is read as end of
    input, which quits.
    Changed from the P8X version: the keys are read without echo and
    Enter may be CR or LF.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/examine.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    dump, dep, mon
